<title>GREECE: GREEK CITIBANK TO CUT BASE LOAN RATE FOR WORKING CAPITAL.</title>
<headline>GREEK CITIBANK TO CUT BASE LOAN RATE FOR WORKING CAPITAL.</headline>
<dateline>ATHENS 1996-08-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>Citibank, Athens will cut its base</p>
<p>lending rate for working capital and public enterprises and</p>
<p>organisations by 25 basis points effective Tuesday, it said in a</p>
<p>statement.</p>
<p>Citibank, which reviews its rates weekly, offers the</p>
<p>following rate structure:</p>
<p>** Base rate for working capital loans to 17.25 percent from</p>
<p>17.50 percent</p>
<p>** Special base rate for public enterprises and</p>
<p>organisations (DEKO) to 18.70 percent from 18.75 percent</p>
<p>** Business and professional base lending rate at 18.75</p>
<p>percent</p>
<p>** Base rate for freely investible funds at 18.50 percent</p>
<p>** Mortgage rate at 18 percent</p>
<p>** Auto loans at 19.75 percent</p>
<p>** Personal installment loans at 24 percent</p>
<p>** Personal installment loans with invoices at 23 percent</p>
<p>Contribution under Law 128 is not included in the rates</p>
<p>quoted, it said.</p>
<p>Rates on working capital loans denominated in foreign</p>
<p>currency for the coming week will be:</p>
<p>    US Dollars     :  8.00 percent</p>
<p>    Marks	    :  5.79 percent</p>
<p>    Yen		:  3.08 percent</p>
<p>    Guilders	 :  5.52 percent</p>
<p>    French francs  :  6.48 percent</p>
<p>    Ecu		:  6.91 percent</p>
<p>Pounds Sterling:  8.34 percent</p>
<p>--Dimitris Kontogiannis, Athens Newsroom +301 3311812-4</p>
</text>
